[38;5;246m[48;5;237m❯ [38;5;231mPeople mostly want to find failed sign-ins and anything that changed their security settings, and to look at one [39m
  [38;5;231mparticular week.[39m
[49m
[38;5;231m⏺[39m That gives three needs: failed sign-ins, security setting changes, and a single week. Failed sign-ins map directly to
  the [38;5;153mSign-in failed[39m type. "Security setting changes" isn't a type, though, so it's the next thing to settle.

  [1mThe choice:[0m whether the page should define a "security changes" group, or let people pick from the nine raw types
  themselves.

  - [1mA. Checkbox list of the nine types only.[0m Someone looking for security changes ticks Password changed, Two-factor
    enabled, and Email change requested themselves. Nothing new gets defined, which fits the Part 1 rule against
    client-side types. The cost is a few extra clicks for the common case. Easy to extend later.
  - [1mB. Checkbox list plus two shortcut buttons, "Failed sign-ins" and "Security changes", that tick a preset set of [0m
    [1mboxes.[0m The common cases take one click, and the checkboxes still show exactly what is selected. The catch is that
    this page would decide what counts as a security change. That's a product decision Security may want a say in, and
    reviewers may question it. The button is cheap to remove later, but its definition would need to be agreed.
  - [1mC. A single grouped Select ("All", "Failed sign-ins", "Security changes", then each type).[0m Most compact. But it
    allows only one choice at a time, so you can't see failed sign-ins and security changes together, and the group
    definition problem from B still applies.

  [1mI recommend B[0m, with "Security changes" defined as [1mPassword changed, Two-factor enabled, and Email change requested[0m.
  "Settings updated" is left out because its [38;5;153mdetail[39m shows it covers non-security fields such as [38;5;153mnotifyDigest[39m. The
  checkboxes keep the shortcut transparent, so nobody is misled about what they're seeing. If Security disagrees with
  the definition, the fix is one line.
